---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PanelHead from '../../components/PanelHead.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Luthier — Andrew Shiau"
  description="A Figma widget that turns a designer’s document into a production template, so nobody has to hand-code one."
>
  <!-- THE SPLIT (chooser round 2: concept 02, scope 03). Same `.pin` /
       `.splitrule` / `.feed` children at the same 4:8 columns as the index, so the
       site's one vertical division doesn't move when you click into a case study. -->
  <div class="grid">
    <PanelHead href="/work/luthier/" title="Luthier">
      <!-- The masthead: metadata you read before the story. On a Type A page the facts block
           is ALWAYS this, never a closing colophon (which is Type B's job) — and in the split
           it is IN the panel, because "before the story" is what a column that never leaves
           the screen means. -->
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Role</div>
        <div class="ftx">
          Sole engineer and designer — <b>frontend, backend, and UX</b>. Ideation,
          requirements, prototyping, usability testing, and delivery.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Stack</div>
        <div class="ftx">React · functional JavaScript · Ruby on Rails · Figma API.</div>
      </div>
    </PanelHead>

    <div class="feed">
      <!-- c1–12 of the FEED's twelve sub-tracks, not c1–8: the dek's own 32em cap is
           narrower than 8 of the feed's 700px, so c1–8 would set a column the text
           never reaches. No top margin — the dek is the top of the reading column. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">
          A Figma widget that turns a designer’s document into a production template, so
          nobody has to hand-code one. Built for Amazon’s marketing-graphics pipeline.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A, beat 2: the clip is the lede (chooser: beats §2 option 03). The page opens
           on the thing working, before any prose. This is the one clip that shows the whole
           idea in one shot — a designer turning a Figma doc into a live template — so it earns
           the position; the other two clips stay down in the mechanics section where they
           explain a detail rather than the premise. -->
      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
        <figure class="demo hang">
          <video src="/media/luthier-04_creating_gateway.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline
            preload="metadata" aria-label="Silent screen recording: the Figma widget creating a Gateway template."></video>
          <figcaption>The Figma widget creating a Gateway template.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>The problem</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              Composer is Amazon-internal software that turns templates into finished
              marketing graphics. It makes more than <b>50,000 images a year</b>, including
              placements on the Amazon Gateway.
            </p>
            <p>
              When I joined, a front-end engineer hand-coded every template. Each one took
              weeks, and nobody but that engineer could change it afterward. To get more
              templates we needed designers who could build one themselves.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="block c1-6">
          <span class="st">Figma is the interface.</span>
          <span class="sd">A designer builds a Figma document. A widget sends it to Composer, which walks every layer and generates the template, the editing UI, and the initial state.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="c1-12"><h2>How it works</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Figma parsing</div>
            <div class="ftx">A Figma→Composer transformer walks the document tree — text, images, shapes — and builds both the template and its editing UI from the layers.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Layout by Figma</div>
            <div class="ftx">It reads Figma’s own layout features (auto-layout, alignment) and converts them to Composer CSS, so designers work in the tools they already use.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Annotations</div>
            <div class="ftx">A small annotation system supplies what Figma can’t infer: which images an end user may replace, which are fixed brand logos, maximum file sizes, text limits.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Modes</div>
            <div class="ftx">Figma Component Sets become template modes — a holiday background, say — and the mode switch is generated for you, even for nested sets.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
        <figure class="demo c1-6">
          <video src="/media/luthier-05b_layout.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline
            preload="metadata" aria-label="Silent screen recording: Figma’s own layout features aligning the elements."></video>
          <figcaption>Figma’s own layout features align the elements.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="demo c7-12">
          <video src="/media/luthier-09_nested_mode.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline
            preload="metadata" aria-label="Silent screen recording: nested Component Sets becoming a mode switch."></video>
          <figcaption>Nested Component Sets → a mode switch, with unneeded UI hidden.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>Engineering</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              Two modules. The <b>transformer</b> reads the Figma document through the REST
              API and converts it to Composer JSON with one pure function,
              <code>getComposerDoc</code>: a tree traversal that collects the layers, the UI,
              and the initial state. The <b>renderer</b> takes that JSON and produces the
              template.
            </p>
            <p>
              I built the schema and the renderer first, to cut down the hand-coding. Figma
              came after: once a template was just a JSON document, anything that could emit
              that document could be the authoring tool.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A, the alternatives beat (round-3 sheet, Q05): what else was on the table and
           why it lost. This is the beat the "Simplest form / Proof" ledger used to stand in
           for — a claim with no rivals reads as a slogan, a rejected option reads as a
           decision. Prose, so it goes under a .shead.
           ONLY sourced alternatives go here. The two below come from the page's own account
           (the status quo it replaced, and the build order I chose). Do not add a third
           without a fact behind it. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>What else was on the table</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              The alternative to all of this was the status quo: keep one engineer hand-coding
              every template. That is what the team was doing, and it capped the number of
              templates at whatever one person could write in a year.
            </p>
            <p>
              The order was also a choice. I could have started with the Figma widget, which
              is the visible half. I built the schema and the renderer first instead, because
              a template that was already a JSON document could accept any authoring tool —
              and if the widget hadn’t worked, the hand-coding would still have gotten faster.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE A close: always a named final section, always last. "Outcome" for finished
           work; "Where it stands" for work still running (see /work/stores-designer/). -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>Outcome</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              I interviewed template designers on five teams (AWS, Alexa, Amazon Business,
              Payments, Fresh), wrote the proposal, and built the transformer, the CRUD app,
              and the backend in <b>12 weeks</b>. <b>Fifteen</b> template designers used the
              beta. What they got stuck on became the roadmap.
            </p>
            <p>
              My team was re-orged in early 2023 and Composer went to another team. I work on
              the same problem now at <a href="/work/stores-designer/">Stores Designer</a>:
              how a lot of teams build one consistent thing without each of them coding it by
              hand.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
